```kotlin
@Module
@InstallIn(SingletonComponent::class)
object NetworkModule {
    @Provides
    @Singleton
    fun provideRetrofit(): Retrofit =
        Retrofit.Builder().baseUrl(BASE_URL).build()
}

class UserRepository @Inject constructor(
    private val api: UserApi  // built from the Retrofit instance above
)

@HiltViewModel
class ProfileViewModel @Inject constructor(
    private val repo: UserRepository
) : ViewModel() { /* ... */ }
```

Notice the chain: `Retrofit` is hand-built once via `@Provides` because Hilt can't construct it on its own; `UserRepository` needs no module at all, since it has an `@Inject` constructor and Hilt can build it directly, resolving its own `UserApi` dependency in turn; `ProfileViewModel` is marked `@HiltViewModel` (required for any ViewModel Hilt injects into) and simply declares it needs a `UserRepository` — Hilt resolves the entire chain automatically from these declarations, with no manual wiring code anywhere connecting them.
